# Rego Policy File — `.rego` Schema
#
# This file documents the authorization policy that `skeleton/agent.rego`
# implements: what engine evaluates it, what input it sees, what decision it
# must produce, and which tokens must be substituted before deployment.
#
# It is an artifact schema — it describes a file that lives in this package,
# not the container format. See `schemas/spec-1/SCHEMATIC.md.schema` for the
# latter.

## File Extension

`.rego` — plain text, Open Policy Agent's policy language. Nothing compiles it
ahead of time: the engine embedded in the authorization plugin parses and
evaluates it.

## Engine and Syntax Version

The policy is evaluated by the OPA engine compiled into the plugin image, so
the plugin release decides the language version. The embedded version was read
from each release's own `go.mod` at its tag, and this package's policy was then
evaluated under all three engines:

| Plugin release | Embedded OPA | Version read from | Result |
|----------------|--------------|-------------------|--------|
| `ghcr.io/open-policy-agent/opa-docker-authz:v0.10` | **v1.3.0** | `go.mod` at tag `v0.10` | loads; every probe row decides as specified |
| `openpolicyagent/opa-docker-authz-v2:0.9` | v0.60.0 | `go.mod` at tag `v0.9` | loads; every probe row decides identically |
| `openpolicyagent/opa-docker-authz-v2:0.8` | v0.30.0 | `go.mod` at tag `v0.8` | not supported — the file fails to load (`import rego.v1` rejected) |

OPA v1.7.1 was run as a forward check: all rows identical. The engine is a
property of the plugin, not of this package, so validate with an engine **no
newer than the plugin's** — a policy that loads on a newer engine may use
syntax the plugin's engine rejects:

```
opa check agent.rego
opa eval -f raw --data agent.rego --input probe.json data.docker.authz.allow
```

A policy that parses is not a policy that decides correctly: see "Probes" below
for the decision checks.

## Placeholders

The file is a template. Every token in the left column must be replaced with
the parameter value in the right column, and **no token may remain** in the
deployed file: with a placeholder left in place, `is_sandbox` never matches a
real client, the policy classifies the sandbox as a host user, and every
request is allowed.

| Token in the file | Parameter | Example value |
|-------------------|-----------|---------------|
| `SANDBOX_USERNAME` | P-4 | `sandbox-agent` |
| `AUTH_HEADER_NAME` | P-9 | `X-Sandbox-Agent` |
| `PROJECT_NAME` | P-3 | `backend-services` |
| `PROJECT_DIR_PATH` | P-15 | `/srv/compose/backend-services` |
| `TESTCONTAINERS_LABEL_KEY` | P-12 | `org.testcontainers` |
| `TESTCONTAINERS_LABEL_VALUE` | P-12 | `true` |
| `EXTRA_BIND_ROOTS` | P-16 | `/srv/media,/srv/downloads` (or empty) |

`BUILDKIT_PREFIX` (P-11) is not read by this file: no rule uses it. The leftover
check below still greps for it, so a copy that carries the token is caught
rather than silently accepted.

The deployed copy must be checked, not assumed — and the check must ignore
comments, which name the tokens on purpose:

```
awk '!/^[[:space:]]*#/ && /SANDBOX_USERNAME|AUTH_HEADER_NAME|PROJECT_NAME|PROJECT_DIR_PATH|EXTRA_BIND_ROOTS|BUILDKIT_PREFIX|TESTCONTAINERS_LABEL/ {print FILENAME":"FNR": "$0}' <deployed>.rego
```

No output means every token that the engine sees was substituted.

## The Input Document

The plugin enriches the request the daemon passes it (Docker's
`AuthZPlugin.AuthZReq` message) before evaluating the policy:

| Field | Type | Meaning |
|-------|------|---------|
| `input.User` | string | The TLS client certificate's subject common name; empty for unix-socket requests and for TCP clients without a certificate |
| `input.AuthMethod` | string | `TLS` when the client authenticated with a certificate |
| `input.Method` | string | HTTP method (`GET`, `HEAD`, `POST`, `DELETE`) |
| `input.Path` | string | Request path with the API version prefix and query string |
| `input.PathPlain` | string | The **raw request path**: API version prefix included, query string excluded (`u.Path`). `/v1.56/containers/create`, not `/containers/create` — nothing strips the version, which is why the policy matches the derived `path` (R-19) instead of this field |
| `input.PathArr` | array | `PathPlain` split on `/` — `["", "v1.56", "containers", "create"]` (plugin addition). Not read by the policy: `path_segments` is the derived path split the same way, so the version element cannot reach a match |
| `input.Query` | object | The parsed query string, as a map of arrays (`{"name": ["web-1"]}`). A container create's name arrives here, not in the body; no grant depends on it |
| `input.Headers` | object | Request headers as a flat `string → string` map — Docker's own message type is `map[string]string`, so a value is a string, never an array |
| `input.Body` | object | Decoded JSON request body, or `null` for requests without one (a `DELETE` carries none) |
| `input.Body.HostConfig` | object | The create's host-side configuration — `Privileged`, `CapAdd`, `Devices`, `SecurityOpt`, `VolumesFrom`, `PidMode`, `IpcMode`, `NetworkMode`, `CgroupnsMode`, `UsernsMode`, `Binds`, `VolumeDriver` (the driver for a `Binds` source that is a volume name rather than a path, read by R-16), `Mounts` (each entry read whole by R-16, `VolumeOptions.DriverConfig.Name` and `.Options` included) — and the whole of the R-15 gate |
| `input.BindMounts` | array | One object per bind mount of a container-create request: `Source`, `ReadOnly`, `Resolved` (plugin addition) |

Every path the policy matches is the derived `path` — `PathPlain` with one
optional `/v<major>[.<minor>]` prefix removed, and with no `..` segment:

```rego
path := p if {
        p := regex.replace(object.get(input, "PathPlain", ""), "^/v[0-9]+(\\.[0-9]+)?", "")
        not traversal(p)
}
path_segments := split(path, "/")
```

`PathPlain` itself is read for nothing else. Fed the real field, this decides the
same as it does for a client that omits the version; fed a version-less field,
the equality rules match nothing — which is why a probe table written against
that shape proves nothing about a live daemon.

`Resolved` is the source path with symlinks resolved, and it is the field that
defeats the documented symlink bypass of bind-mount checks — but the plugin can
only resolve paths it can read. A managed-plugin install mounts only the policy
directory into the plugin, so `Resolved` is empty for project paths; a legacy
install (plugin container started with the host filesystem readable) or a
custom plugin `config.json` is what makes it useful. The policy therefore checks
every witness of a mount — `Resolved`, `Source`, and the raw
`HostConfig.Binds`/`HostConfig.Mounts` arrays — rather than the first that
answers, and SCHEMATIC.md states which of them is effective in which deployment
shape.

## The Decision

- The policy must be in package `docker.authz`; the plugin queries the `allow`
  rule (`data.docker.authz.allow`).
- `true` allows the request, `false` denies it, and an undefined/empty result
  denies it (the engine treats a missing decision as denial).
- A denial reaches the client as
  `Error response from daemon: authorization denied by plugin <name>: ...`.
- The policy is pure: same input, same decision, no side effects, no state.

## Probes

The decisions this package relies on, each an `opa eval` against the deployed
file with a hand-built input. Every row below was run under OPA v0.60.0, v1.3.0,
and v1.7.1 (the two embedded engines plus a newer one), and every row decides as
its Expected column says on all three.

**Every row's input carries the plugin's real field values** — `Path` and
`PathPlain` with the API version prefix, `PathArr` split from that, `Query`
parsed — because `main.go`'s `makeInput` builds them from the raw request URL
and nothing strips the version. A table built on a version-less `PathPlain`
proves nothing about a live daemon, and the two shapes disagree in both
directions:

- **A version-less `PathPlain`** makes an equality rule match a path the plugin
  never sends, so rows the policy must deny appear allowed, and the rows that
  show the grant working are absent.
- **The real `PathPlain`** leaves every equality rule unmatched, so the grants
  vanish: creates, volume and network creates, deletes, lifecycle actions,
  build, pull and `/session` all decide `false`, and the failure is easy to miss
  because it fails **closed** — every denial still holds.

Run the table whenever the shape is in doubt, and confirm the Expected column
of **every** row; a table that agrees with itself is not evidence.

Each row is one `opa eval` against the substituted policy:

```
opa eval -f raw --data agent.rego --input <row>.json data.docker.authz.allow
```

evaluated with the engine the plugin embeds (P-10) — a container image of that
engine does when no matching `opa` binary is available. The version prefix the
rows carry is a parameter of whatever builds them, not of the policy: run the
table twice when the shape is in question, once with a prefix and once without,
and require identical decisions. Then run at least one **live** test (SCHEMATIC.md's A-11 or
A-16): a table cannot disagree with the plugin if it is fed the plugin's values,
but nothing but a live call proves those values are the plugin's.

The rows are also a regression suite: they are listed so a future change cannot
quietly break them.

The `<P-3>` token below stands for the project name (`backend-services` with the
defaults), `<P-15>` for the project directory (`/srv/compose/backend-services`),
and every row is evaluated with the identity `User: <P-4>` unless it says
otherwise.

`<P-16>` stands for the extra bind roots of the deployment a row describes. The
rows that name an accepted extra root are evaluated with `P-16` set
to `/srv/media,/srv/data/known_hosts` — deliberately a directory **and** a single
file — and **every other row in this file is evaluated with `P-16` empty**, which
is the parameter's default. State the value a row used when you re-run it; a row
that grants a bind proves nothing about the default deployment.

### R-15 — a create must not reach the host

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `POST /containers/create`, project label, `HostConfig{Privileged:true, Binds:["/:/host"], PidMode:"host"}` | deny |
| same, project label, `CapAdd: ["SYS_ADMIN"]` | deny |
| same, project label, `Devices: [{PathOnHost:"/dev/kvm"}]` | deny |
| same, project label, `SecurityOpt: ["seccomp=unconfined"]` | deny |
| same, project label, `VolumesFrom: ["host-container"]` | deny |
| same, project label, `PidMode: "host"` | deny |
| same, project label, `IpcMode: "host"` | deny |
| same, project label, `NetworkMode: "host"` | deny |
| same, project label, `NetworkMode: "container:<id>"` | deny — a joined namespace is the host's, one hop away |
| same, project label, `CgroupnsMode: "host"` | deny |
| same, project label, `UsernsMode: "host"` | deny |
| same, project label, `Binds: ["/var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock"]` | deny |
| same, project label, `Binds: ["/etc:/host-etc"]` | deny |
| same, project label, `Binds: ["<P-15>/../../etc:/x"]` | deny — the path starts with `<P-15>` but carries a `..` segment |
| same, project label, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"bind", Source:"/root", Target:"/root"}]` | deny |
| same, project label, `BindMounts: [{Source:"<P-15>/link", Resolved:"/etc"}]` | deny — the symlink case `Resolved` exists for |
| same, project label, `BindMounts: [{Source:"/etc"}]`, no `HostConfig` mounts at all | deny — the plugin's enrichment alone is enough to refuse |
| same with a *testcontainers* label instead, `Privileged: true` | deny — the gate covers every create grant |
| `POST /containers/create` with a BuildKit container name and `Privileged: true` | deny — the name-prefix carve-out is gone (R-17) |
| `POST /containers/create`, no label, no carve-out | deny — attribution is by label |
| `POST /containers/create`, no label, but `Binds: ["<P-15>/src:/app/src"]` | deny — a project *path* is not attribution either |
| `POST /containers/create`, label of another project | deny |
| `POST /containers/create`, project label, no body | deny |
| `POST /containers/<id>/attach`, body `{Name:"/buildx_buildkit_"}` | deny — a create-shaped body on another endpoint satisfies nothing |
| `POST /containers/<id>/exec`, body `{Name:"/buildx_buildkit_", Cmd:["sh"]}` | deny — same, and exec is refused outright (R-6) |
| `POST /containers/create`, project label, no `HostConfig` at all | allow — the ordinary compose create |
| same, empty `HostConfig: {}` | allow |
| same, `Binds: ["<P-15>/src:/app/src"]` | allow — a host path inside `<P-15>` |
| same, `Binds: ["<P-3>_data:/var/lib/data"]` | allow — a named volume |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"volume", Source:"<P-3>_data", Target:"/data"}]` | allow |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"volume", Source:"fresh-name", Target:"/host", VolumeOptions:{DriverConfig:{Name:"local", Options:{type:"none", o:"bind", device:"/"}}}}]` | deny — the mount's own driver options are a bind of `/`: the daemon applies them when it creates the named volume, and the local driver then binds the device. The same capability R-16 refuses at `/volumes/create` |
| same, that row with `device:"/etc"` | deny — any non-empty option set, not only `/` |
| same, that row with `device:"<P-15>"` | deny — fail-closed on purpose: options naming a path *inside* the project directory are refused too, because a gate that accepted them would be maintaining a copy of the local driver's option grammar |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"volume", Source:"fresh-name", Target:"/host", VolumeOptions:{DriverConfig:{Name:"evil"}}}]` | deny — a driver other than the local one, with no options at all |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"volume", Source:"fresh-name", Target:"/host", VolumeOptions:{DriverConfig:{Name:"local", Options:{}}}}]` | allow — an explicitly empty option set is no options |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"volume", Source:"fresh-name", Target:"/host", VolumeOptions:{}}]` | allow — `VolumeOptions` that names no driver and passes no options |
| same, `Binds: ["<P-3>_data:/data"]`, `HostConfig.VolumeDriver: "evil"` | deny — the legacy `Binds` route takes its driver from this field, and another driver's volume is one the policy cannot see the contents of |
| same, `Binds: ["<P-3>_data:/data"]`, `HostConfig.VolumeDriver: "local"` | allow — naming the local driver is what the absent field means |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"bind", Source:"<P-15>/x", Target:"/x"}]` | allow |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Source:"<P-15>/x", Target:"/x"}]` (no `Type`) | allow — an untyped entry is treated as a bind |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Source:"/etc", Target:"/etc"}]` (no `Type`) | deny |
| same, `BindMounts: [{Source:"<P-15>/src"}]` (no `Resolved`) | allow — the absent-field case must not refuse a legitimate mount |
| same, `BindMounts: [{Source:"<P-15>/src", Resolved:""}]` | allow — the managed-plugin shape, where `Resolved` cannot be filled |
| same, `BindMounts: [{Source:"/etc"}]` (no `Resolved`) | deny |
| testcontainers label, no `HostConfig` | allow |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/media/downloads:/data"]` | allow — a bind source inside an accepted extra root |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/media:/data"]` | allow — the root itself |
| same, `HostConfig.Mounts: [{Type:"bind", Source:"/srv/media/lib", Target:"/lib"}]` | allow |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/data/known_hosts:/known:ro"]` | allow — a single file named exactly in `P-16` |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/data/other"]` | deny — naming the file does not grant the directory holding it |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/mediaX:/x"]` | deny — a root is a path boundary, not a string prefix |
| same, `Binds: ["/srv/media/../../etc:/x"]` | deny — traversal under an accepted root; this is what a prefix grant otherwise reaches |
| same, `BindMounts: [{Source:"/srv/media/x", Resolved:"/etc"}]` | deny — the symlink case still applies inside an extra root |

### R-17 — grants match a path exactly

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `POST /build` | allow |
| `POST /build/prune` | deny — no match by prefix |
| `POST /session` | allow — the BuildKit CLI session R-7's build needs |
| `POST /containers/create?name=buildx_buildkit_default0`, no label | deny — a `name` query parameter attributes nothing, and the carve-out is gone |
| `GET /_ping` with no version prefix at all | allow — read-only, and nothing to strip (a `HEAD /_ping` never reaches the policy: `-skip-ping` defaults to true) |
| `POST /containers/create` with the project label, **no** version prefix | allow — a client that omits the version is not a different client |
| `POST /v1.56/v1.56/containers/create` with the project label | deny — one strip only, and the result is not a granted path |
| `POST /v1.56/containers/../containers/create` with the project label | deny — a `..` segment means no `path`, so no rule matches |
| `POST /v1.56/containers/../../volumes/<P-3>_data` | deny — same guard, on a path-named volume operation |
| `DELETE /v1.56/volumes/../volumes/hostdata` | deny — same guard, on a delete |
| `POST /v1/containers/create` with the project label (major only) | allow — the minor is optional in the strip; the daemon's own router still decides whether it routes |

### R-12 — creation is scoped by name

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `POST /networks/create`, `{Name:"<P-3>_default"}` | allow |
| `POST /networks/create`, `{Name:"other_default"}` | deny |

### R-16 — a volume create may not be a host mount

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `POST /volumes/create`, `{Name:"<P-3>_root", Driver:"local", DriverOpts:{type:"none", o:"bind", device:"/"}}` | deny |
| same, `{Name:"<P-3>_net", Driver:"nfs"}` | deny |
| same, `{Name:"<P-3>_data", Driver:"local"}` | allow |
| same, `{Name:"<P-3>_cache"}` (no driver) | allow |
| same, `{Name:"other_data"}` | deny — a foreign name |

### R-18 — deletes are scoped by name

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `DELETE /volumes/<P-3>_data` | allow |
| `DELETE /networks/<P-3>_default` | allow |
| `DELETE /volumes/hostdata` | deny |
| `DELETE /networks/hostnet` | deny |
| `DELETE /volumes/<64-hex id>` | deny — a delete that names the resource by id cannot be attributed; see Limitations |
| `DELETE /containers/<id>` | allow — documented limitation: no attribution is possible |

### Regressions — the rest of the contract

| Input | Expected |
|-------|----------|
| `User: ""` (unix socket), any method, including a privileged create | allow — host users are unrestricted (R-10) |
| `User: <P-4>`, `GET /containers/json` | allow — read-only grant (R-4) |
| `User: <P-4>`, `HEAD /containers/json` | allow |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /images/create` | allow — pull (R-7) |
| `User: <P-4>`, `DELETE /images/<id>` | deny |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /secrets/create` | deny |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /containers/<id>/start` | allow — lifecycle allowlist (R-8) |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /containers/<id>/stop` | allow |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /containers/<id>/kill` | allow — an existing container, any id; see Limitations |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /containers/<id>/update` | allow — same |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /networks/<P-3>_default/connect` | allow (R-12) |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /networks/other_default/connect` | deny |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /volumes/<P-3>_data` (path-named operation) | allow |
| `User: <P-4>`, `POST /volumes/other_data` | deny |
| `User: <other>`, header `<P-9>: true`, `GET /containers/json` | allow — the header is the secondary identity |
| `User: <other>`, header `<P-9>: true`, create with another project's label | deny — the header narrows to the sandbox, it does not grant a project |
| `User: <other>` without the header, any method | allow — a non-sandbox identity is a host user |

